ay  the rail strike the impact and what  happens next  well what does that say you know i mean  it's always a failure like if you get to  the point where there's a strike  everyone has failed management has  failed the unions have failed in this  case also the government has failed  because despite its repeated claims that  it can't go anywhere near the  negotiating table it is of course  the decisions are contingent on the  decisions it makes the controls the kind  of funding you're looking for national  rail it needs to be there and yet it's  done nothing to help the situation and  in fact has spent most of the last two  weeks as far as i can tell  making social media  memes about kia stamina somehow being  responsible for the strike so it seems  to be sort of this quite unseemly way  almost enthusiastic about the strikes  let alone being able to do anything to  help  there's very little sign at the moment  that it's going to be any better for the  rest of the week and like i say that is  a failure in pretty much every direction  you can consider  well indeed so and um you know the  impact being lessened or cushioned by  you know everybody's experiences during  the pandemic um working from home where  they can you know does that drag the  strike out does that mean  mean that the real action by the union  um is neutered in any way does it have  any impact at all anita  well i would say that i'm struck by the  fact that normally the papers following  a strike day would have pictures of sort  of  empty london and and old people kind of  enormous cues of people on for buses and  that kind of thing but we've seen i  can't see any of that on the front pages  so it might be an indication that  obviously office workers who can often  work from home and are choosing to have  a hybrid model mostly in um in in  business that we're actually seeing that  reflected through in response to days  like today but can i just respond to  some of what ian was saying about the  government's role in this i do sort of  wonder when people say well the  government needs to do more that  essentially means the government needs  to put some money on the table  and it's clear from the r t's  expectations which seem to be sort of  around seven percent and and what bosses  are being offered which is around three  percent as reported in the ft that  that's quite a big kind of golf and the  role that um the government would play  would basically be in funding and it  feels to me as we've just been talking  about the kind of hybrid working that  the ways in which we use the rail  service in general are going to change  and that really has to be engaged with  and it's i'm not i'm not convinced i'm  not entirely sure what people mean when  they say the government needs to do more  because because that feels to me like it  just means the government needs to give  um  just to provide additional funding to  either i know national rail etc to be  able to pay the workers more and prevent  the kind of redundancies that are going  to be needed to reshape um the rail  service for the future  yeah i mean this is this is not just  about payers there's been a lot of  coverage of of pay inflation matching  pay required but a lot of this is about  changing to working conditions the  sunday times talking about the closure  of all ticket offices which makes  consumers sit up and take notice as well  so how much is this about the need for  progress to increase um  you know effect  productivity i suppose is the best way  to describe it ian um and how much is  you know is the criticism of the union  being luddite effectively a genuine one  well their financial claim i think is  relatively modest it's still four points  below inflation inflation is about 11.  they're asking for seven percent i mean  i do think that's modest and i think  that's reasonable in its own terms when  you get into more dangerous territory is  with reluctance to adopt new practices  now we don't really know what the  details are of this but it's certainly  true the unions are quite reticent about  giving you information yes on ticket  offices but also on things that seem  more reasonable for instance like the  use of drones to assess tracks all of  that of course can have an impact on  members and sometimes unions are well  within their rights to complain about  them but looking at it from a sort of  broader societal point of view you think  well that's the kind of thing you could  do  so yes it can go both ways the financial  claim might be stronger some of the  working conditions claims might not be  so strong the trouble is it's not really  about the claims themselves it's about  how viable a negotiation you have if you  have a party that is key to the  decisions that are made and the  government is key to it we all  understand on any political analysis but  part of the reason the government wants  to be tough on this strike is because it  cannot afford to be seen to be soft on  it knowing that it's got teacher strikes  potentially coming down the tracks that  barristers are about to go on strikes  that there's probably going to be male  strikes that we're essentially in the  beginning of the summer of discontent so  on that basis if you've got a room with  two negotiating partners but in reality  there is a third partner who is not in  the room and pretending that it has  nothing to do with it while in fact  being completely contingent for the  outcome that doesn't seem like the most  conducive environment to get the result  that you want well certainly the sun  picks up on what you were saying about  the teaching unions uh saying the  national education union  has told number 10 it wants 12 pay rises  for its members by september at 12  um and we move on if we if you don't  mind to the metro which is talking about  a double-digit increase  in pensions  for older people retirees set to see  double-digit payments increases next  year as the state pension will be  